    "translation": "At convenience stores, where being open at all hours was once taken for granted, a movement to reconsider 24-hour operation is spreading. Behind it lies a serious labor shortage. Securing people to work late at night grows harder every year, and personnel costs keep rising. In areas with few late-night customers, there are reportedly quite a few stores whose losses grow the longer they stay open.\nOne major chain introduced a system allowing franchise stores to suspend late-night operation at their own discretion. According to reports on the experiment, even when stores closed late at night, daily sales did not fall very much; on the contrary, the burden on employees lightened and fewer people quit. On the other hand, there are cautious voices. Convenience stores also serve as supply points for goods in times of disaster and as bases for nighttime crime prevention, so ending late-night operation could undermine the community's sense of security.\nNeither convenience nor workers' health can easily be given up. In a society whose population is shrinking, however, we probably cannot keep maintaining every service just as before. What do we keep, and what do we give up? The debate over convenience stores' late-night hours asks how our society will come to terms with convenience itself.",
