    "translation": "With the progress of artificial intelligence—AI—we often hear anxious voices asking whether our jobs will be taken away. Certainly, AI has already come to handle routine tasks such as data entry and document checking faster and more accurately than humans. According to one survey, about half of workers in Japan believe that part of their own job will be automated in the future.\nLooking back at history, however, technological progress has not only erased jobs. The spread of the automobile did reduce work related to horse-drawn carriages, but it created new jobs—drivers, mechanics, and others—that outnumbered those lost. The same can be said of AI. Occupations that never existed before are already emerging, such as designing business processes that make full use of AI, or verifying whether AI's judgments are appropriate.\nWhat is being asked is not how to beat AI, but how to divide the work with it. Even if we cannot match machines in calculation or memory, reading other people's feelings and tackling unprecedented problems remain fields where humans excel. Rather than looking away from the anxiety, we should think about which parts of our work to entrust to machines and where to pour human strength. That, surely, is the attitude required of workers from now on.",
