    "translation": "At Japanese companies, employees holding other jobs—so-called side jobs—were long regarded as undesirable, and most companies prohibited them in their work regulations. In recent years, however, as the government began encouraging side jobs as part of its work-style reforms, the number of companies permitting them has been growing rapidly.\nSeveral circumstances lie behind this change in corporate attitudes. First is sluggish wage growth. With sufficient raises hard to provide, companies have had no choice but to allow employees a means of supplementing their income. Second is the expectation of developing human resources. If employees who gain new knowledge and personal connections through experience outside the company bring those gains back to their main job, the company loses nothing. In fact, companies that have permitted side jobs report that their employees' horizons have broadened and their motivation for work has risen.\nOf course, this is not without problems. If the combined working hours of the main and side jobs grow too long, workers' health could suffer. Some also worry about the risk of information leaking outside. Whether side jobs can be turned into growth for workers, rather than ending as mere pocket money, depends, one could say, on how well both companies and employees can manage time and keep a clear sense of purpose.",
