    "translation": "This book is a novel depicting one year in the life of its forty-year-old protagonist, who quits a Tokyo publishing house and eventually opens a bakery in a small seaside town. The author has published many works featuring women working in the city, but this is reportedly her first full-length novel set in the countryside.\nThe first half of the story consists of scene after scene of the protagonist walking around town looking for a place to open her shop. To be honest, I felt this part was somewhat too long. From the middle section, however, when she encounters the building of a closed-down tofu shop, I was drawn into the story all at once. The negotiations with the taciturn landlord and the way the distance between her and the local people gradually shrinks are depicted carefully, in a style that never feels preachy. In particular, the depiction of the dusk on the day she first sells out of bread is probably the most beautiful scene in the book.\nThough it is a story of a dream coming true, the book does not lean on the sweetness of a success story. The author does not hide the nights when the protagonist worries about sales and wavers over returning to the city. Readers come to worry together with the protagonist and to rejoice together in her small successes. That is precisely why the final line leaves such a deep resonance. Despite the flaw of a slow first half, this is a book I want to recommend to everyone who is unsure about how to work and live.",
