    "translation": "The film \"Voice of the Mountain,\" released last week, portrays the life of a real woman who took up mountain climbing after passing the age of seventy. It is based on her bestselling memoir of the same title, but rather than simply lining up the facts, the director rebuilt the story around the protagonist's memories of her late husband. It is fair to say this decision succeeds.\nWhat deserves praise first is the lead actress's performance. Even in scenes with few lines, she conveys the character's inner movements through expression and breath alone; in the scene where she takes out her husband's hat at the summit, quiet sobbing could be heard around the theater. The cinematography capturing the mountain scenery is also superb, giving the audience the sensation of standing on the slopes themselves.\nOn the other hand, it is not without faults. The second half, which depicts the conflict with her daughter, has more and more expository dialogue, and compared with the restrained storytelling of the first half it left a somewhat ordinary impression. The running time of two and a half hours is also long; several of the flashback scenes along the way could surely have been cut.\nEven so, there are not many films that make you think about how to carry your own years after watching. If possible, I hope you will see it on a big theater screen. This is one of the best films of the year, one I can recommend both to those who have read the memoir and to those who start with the film.",
