    "translation": "In recent years, the phrase 'tourism pollution' has come to be heard in famous sightseeing spots. With the rapid increase in foreign visitors, more and more residents cannot board their buses, or find their daily lives disrupted by litter, noise, and crowds. In one city famous for its old streets, the municipal buses that residents rely on became so packed with tourists that the city had to step in with countermeasures. Tourism is an important industry that brings money to a region, but if its profits rest on the sacrifice of residents' lives, that is putting the cart before the horse.\nSome cities have begun experiments such as levying accommodation taxes or making entry to popular spots reservation-only. There is criticism — 'Are we really going to turn away visitors who came all this way?' — but I think the opposite is true. Tourists will eventually stop coming to a destination that has lost its charm to overcrowding. Restrictions are an investment to protect the value of the destination.\nWhat is even more important is finding ways to keep travelers from concentrating in particular places and seasons. If we promote the appeal of lesser-known regions and spread out the timing and destinations of travel, the burden on residents will shrink and the regions that benefit will multiply. Discounts encouraging weekday or winter travel would also be effective. Balancing tourism and daily life is by no means impossible.",
