    "translation": "Hoping to halt rural population decline, many municipalities have aimed to attract new permanent residents. But there is a limit to a structure in which municipalities nationwide fight over a limited pool of would-be migrants. What has drawn attention in recent years is the idea of the 'related population': people who do not relocate, but who visit a particular region repeatedly and stay involved with it — helping at festivals, buying local specialties, and so on.\nThe advantage of the related population is its low barrier to entry. Many people who cannot move because of work or family could still manage a visit once a month. In fact, there is said to be a company employee who, visiting every year to help with rice planting, came to build an almost family-like relationship with the local people. The region, for its part, gains outside perspectives and labor without increasing the burden of resident services.\nThat said, if a region treats its related population as mere 'convenient helpers,' the relationship will not last. What matters is building a relationship in which the region and the individual benefit mutually, on equal footing — one, for example, where outsiders' ideas feed into developing local specialties and successes are celebrated together. Rather than chasing population figures, nurture the quality of involvement. I believe the future of rural areas depends on that shift in thinking.",
