    "translation": "Japan's food self-sufficiency rate remains just under forty percent on a calorie basis, strikingly low even among developed countries. In other words, more than sixty percent of what is on our tables depends on imports. In normal times this causes no inconvenience at all, but if imports stall because of shifts in international affairs or extreme weather, food prices shoot up at once. Indeed, the recent surge in grain prices hit household budgets directly, in the form of higher prices for bread and noodles. What is more, with farmers aging, domestic production capacity itself is thinning year by year.\nAgainst the argument that the self-sufficiency rate should be raised, there is the counterargument that relying on cheap imports is more rational. If one thinks only of economic efficiency, that may indeed be so. But food is unlike other goods: once it runs out, it is too late to start looking for a new supplier.\nOf course, covering everything with domestic production is unrealistic. What I want to argue is this: while accepting dependence on imports as a premise, we should maintain the production base for things we can grow at home, such as rice and vegetables, and preserve the capacity to increase output in an emergency. It is like insurance — it looks like waste, but it is in fact a safeguard that supports our daily lives.",
