    "translation": "In rural areas, one bus route after another is being discontinued. Ridership has fallen with the shrinking population, and the driver shortage is severe, so loss-making routes can no longer be maintained. Indeed, in my own hometown last year, a route that had run for half a century disappeared, transforming the scenery of the school commute. Once the buses are gone, elderly people and high school students who cannot drive lose their means of getting to the hospital or to school. 'Freedom of movement' is the foundation of daily life, and its loss accelerates a region's decline.\nDrawing attention as a countermeasure is demand-responsive transport, which runs according to reservations. Instead of sending an empty bus out at fixed times, operating small vehicles to match riders' bookings reduces waste and keeps costs down. Still, a problem remains: it is hard to use for elderly people unaccustomed to booking by phone or app. For this reason, some municipalities have set up desks that make the reservations on riders' behalf.\nI believe the era of judging regional transport solely by whether it turns a profit is over. Like roads and water supply, means of transport are infrastructure that sustains a region. Government and residents should share the costs and combine buses, demand-responsive transport, and shared taxis to build a system suited to each region.",
