    "translation": "Now that people speak of a 'hundred-year life,' interest in adult 'relearning' is rising. In an age of rapid technological progress, it is hard to keep working for more than forty years on nothing but the knowledge acquired at school. Indeed, the number of company employees attending graduate school at night or on weekends, and of people studying new fields through online courses, is steadily growing. The government, too, is backing this movement with subsidies.\nYet surveys show that the time Japanese working adults spend on learning is remarkably short by international standards. Overtime on weekdays and housework on days off leave no time at a desk — such voices are certainly common. Long working hours are the reason usually given, but I do not think that is all. What truly drains motivation, I suspect, is that what one learns rarely leads to promotion or a better job — there is no mechanism by which the effort pays off.\nIf so, what is needed is not more courses but a framework for evaluating learning. If companies came to give fair weight to the fruits of relearning in hiring and promotion, people would naturally begin to study. Relearning is not a personal hobby; it is an investment in society as a whole.",
