    "translation": "'Young people these days are reluctant to spend,' we often hear. Surveys do show that they have little interest in cars or luxury goods and put much of their income into savings and investment. A life of skipping travel and dining out and spending days off watching free videos is no longer unusual. Compared with the young of the bubble era, who consumed enthusiastically, some critics say today's youth have gone 'on the defensive.'\nBut dismissing this change as a matter of young people's character misses the point. They grew up in an era when lifetime employment is no longer a given, and they carry anxieties about the pension system. There is no guarantee their incomes will rise, and the share working in non-regular jobs is high. With no visible guarantee for the future, trying to provide for themselves is, if anything, a rational decision.\nOn the other hand, seen from the standpoint of society as a whole, stagnant consumption risks shrinking the economy. Lecturing the young to 'spend more' is pointless. What is needed is to stabilize employment and reduce anxiety about the future — for example, policies that widen the path to regular employment or lighten the burden of housing costs. When a sense of security returns, purse strings loosen of their own accord. We should take young people's inclination to save as a warning from them to society.",
