    "translation": "The 'standard family' in Japan once meant a family of four: a couple and their children. Today, however, the most common household is the single-person household, now approaching forty percent of all households. Behind this lie rising rates of people never marrying, an aging population, and a growing number of elderly people who have lost a spouse. The same trend is advancing not only in cities but in rural areas as well.\nThe increase in single households forces a rethink of society's arrangements, because so many systems — housing, food portions, even public services — were designed on the premise of family living. Indeed, the market has already begun to change: supermarkets line their shelves with small-portion packs, and small dwellings for singles are multiplying.\nMore serious is the problem of isolation. With family, people can help one another in illness or disaster; living alone, they cannot. Yet this is not something solved by urging people to marry. Society itself must prepare arrangements through which people support one another loosely — through friends and neighborhoods — without depending on blood ties. A registry through which neighbors check on one another in disasters, or a community dining hall where people can share meals, would be a first step. Living alone and being isolated are not the same thing.",
