    "translation": "In regional cities whose populations keep shrinking, the idea of the 'compact city' is drawing attention. It is a vision of gathering the houses and shops that have sprawled into the suburbs back around train stations and the like, rebuilding towns small enough to live in on foot. If residents remain dispersed, the costs of maintaining roads and water systems, running buses, and clearing snow weigh heavily and squeeze municipal finances.\nAs logic, it is easy to follow. Realizing it, however, is not easy. Residents' feeling that they do not want to leave homes they have lived in for years is only natural, and forced relocation is out of the question. Some also feel that letting go of suburban land or a shop would mean losing the very memories of their lives there. There are also reports of land prices rising in city centers, making them, if anything, harder to live in.\nEven so, I believe the direction itself is right. What matters is taking time — on the scale of decades. Place hospitals and libraries in the center and draw people in with convenience. Do not abruptly abandon the lives of those who remain in the suburbs. Vacated suburban land can also be put to use as farmland or parks. An attitude of 'regrowing' the town rather than 'folding it up' will be the key to winning residents' acceptance.",
