    "translation": "The hometown tax program is a system in which donating to a municipality you wish to support reduces, by that amount, the tax you pay to the town where you live. Since donors often receive local specialties as thank-you gifts, users have grown year by year, and the program has channeled substantial funds to rural areas. In not a few cases, producers have found new sales channels and a town's name has become known nationwide. The program is also used as a way to donate to towns struck by disaster.\nAt the same time, problems have become clear. Urban municipalities cry out that tax revenue is draining away and they cannot maintain the level of resident services. Moreover, competition to attract donations with lavish gifts has overheated, and the reality is closer to 'shopping' than to 'support.' It has also been pointed out that much of each donation vanishes into the cost of procuring the gifts. The criterion for choosing where to donate has become the value of the gift, not sympathy for the region.\nI would not go so far as to say the system should be abolished. But the mechanisms for stating clearly how donations are used and reporting the results should be strengthened. If you learned that your donation paid for children's school lunches or revived a festival, you would want to donate again next year even without a gift. Is that not what 'supporting your hometown' was originally meant to be?",
