    "translation": "Pay a monthly fee and you can listen to all the music and watch all the films you want. In recent years, flat-rate rental services have spread to clothes, furniture, even cars. This arrangement, called the 'subscription,' symbolizes a shift in consumption 'from owning to using.'\nFor users, the appeal is being able to try many options while keeping initial costs down. For people who move often, renting furniture keeps them light on their feet, and there is the ease of quitting once you tire of something. For companies, too, there are advantages: stable monthly revenue and the ability to improve services based on usage data.\nBut convenience has its pitfalls. A surprising number of people keep paying every month for services they have joined but no longer use. Each one is a small sum, but together they add up to a large outlay. I regard the subscription as a form of consumption that tests one's 'ability to manage contracts.' Review your usage regularly and cancel what you do not use. Only for those who can take on that chore is this arrangement truly a good deal.",
