    "translation": "With the spread of smartphone flea-market apps, buying and selling secondhand goods between individuals is no longer anything special. Clothes no longer worn and books already read turn into money with a few minutes' tapping, and the things you want can be had cheaper than new. Among the young especially, 'checking secondhand first' before buying anything is becoming the norm.\nWhat is interesting is that this habit is changing how people buy in the first place. More and more people, assuming they will 'sell it when they are done,' choose items that will resell well even if they cost a little more. With resale in mind, they keep the box and the manual instead of throwing them away, and handle their belongings with care. The generation once said to be 'drifting away from things' may in fact be acquiring the art of a long relationship with them.\nSome companies no doubt worry that when secondhand goods sell, new ones do not. But I do not think this current should be seen as a decline of consumption. One item going on being used as it changes hands is a turn away from a society that produces in bulk and discards in bulk. Companies, too, should answer this change — with products that last and are easy to repair, and with buy-back services for used goods.",
