    "translation": "The amount of rice Japanese people eat in a year has fallen to roughly half of what it was half a century ago. Bread for breakfast and noodles for lunch is now nothing unusual. A major factor is that, with the rise of dual-income households, the very chore of washing and cooking rice has come to be avoided. Bread or cereal can be eaten right away even on a busy morning.\nThe effects of the shift away from rice reach beyond the dining table. When rice consumption falls, paddies cannot be maintained, and farmers keep abandoning cultivation. Paddies do more than produce food: they store water during heavy rains to prevent floods, and they preserve beautiful scenery and habitats for living things. That rice does not sell means these workings of the land itself are being lost.\nSome say the diversification of diet is a natural current that cannot be stopped. I think that is true. What I want to propose is not forcing people to 'cook and eat' rice, but changing rice itself to fit today's life: packed rice you need only microwave, bread and sweets made with rice flour, greater use in restaurants and boxed lunches. If rice goes on being eaten even in altered forms, the paddies will be preserved. Tradition survives by changing its shape.",
