    "translation": "Convenience stores, construction sites, nursing homes. These days not a day passes without seeing foreign workers. Against the backdrop of a severe labor shortage, their numbers keep growing, and the system is changing to let them work longer and live here with their families. They are no longer temporary helpers; they have become a pillar supporting Japanese society.\nAnd yet the preparations on the receiving side have not kept up. Places to learn Japanese are scarce, and in many regions the task still rests on volunteers' goodwill. There are reports of people refused apartments simply for being foreign, and of children who receive no Japanese-language support at school and cannot keep up in class. This is the result of welcoming them as labor while failing to build the arrangements to receive them as residents.\nWhat must not be forgotten is the fact that it is they who choose the country they work in. As wage gaps narrow, people will flow toward countries that are easier to work and live in. Organizing Japanese-language education as a government responsibility, and expanding support for housing, medical care, and children's schooling — that is not a kindness done for foreigners, but an investment in Japan itself, so that it goes on being chosen by those who sustain its society.",
