    "translation": "\"The Mystery of Sleep,\" which I read last week, is a book that introduces the frontiers of sleep research to general readers. The author is a scientist who has studied sleep for many years, but instead of lining up technical terms, he explains things using familiar examples, so you can keep reading even without prior knowledge. At the end of each chapter the key points are summarized, so it also suits busy people reading a little at a time.\nWhat left the strongest impression on me was the chapter saying that the brain organizes memories during sleep. While we sleep we appear to be doing nothing, but in fact the brain is sorting the information gained during the day and moving what is needed into long-term memory. The experimental finding that you score better on tests after sleeping well than after staying up all night studying was persuasive.\nOn the other hand, the later chapters become rather technical, and the sections full of data explanations were, frankly, boring. Even so, the author's words — \"cutting your sleep is nothing more than borrowing time from your tomorrow's self\" — deserve to reach many people as a warning to modern people who tend to put off sleep because they are busy. This is a book I would especially like people who dislike science books to pick up.",
