    "translation": "I saw \"The Town Library,\" a documentary that recorded a small port town's library over three years. Nothing dramatic happens. The head librarian shelves books, a regular elderly patron reads the newspaper, children stop by after school to do homework. Just that everyday life is carefully captured by a quiet camera. Almost no music plays; all you hear is the sound of waves and of pages turning.\nTo be honest, I got a little sleepy during the first half. But in the second half, from the scene where closing the library is considered because of the town's shrinking population, the film suddenly tightens. Watching the residents try to protect the library not as \"a place to borrow books\" but as \"a place where you can meet people\" made me think about what the true value of such a facility is. Especially memorable was a high-school girl collecting signatures against the closure. For her, the library was a third place to belong — neither home nor school.\nThe director uses almost no narration, letting the residents' expressions and silences do the talking. That restrained technique, if anything, stimulates the audience's imagination. It is a film that leaves you with questions rather than giving you answers. It is showing in only a few theaters, but if you get the chance, I urge you to see it on the big screen.",
