    "translation": "I went to the ukiyo-e exhibition now being held at the municipal art museum. What makes this exhibition distinctive is that it shows not only finished works but also the production process. Preparatory sketches, woodblocks, and artisans' tools are on display, and you can clearly see that a single ukiyo-e print is born not from the artist alone but from the divided labor of many artisans. For instance, I was surprised to learn that nearly ten woodblocks may go into a single work, with a separate block carved for each color.\nIn the latter half of the venue, they used side-by-side comparisons of actual prints to show how the same design can leave a very different impression depending on how the colors are layered. The explanation that ukiyo-e is less a \"painting\" than a \"printed object,\" and that it was precisely because many copies could be made that ordinary people could buy them cheaply, struck me as convincing. Imagining that people of the time enjoyed ukiyo-e the way we enjoy magazines and posters today suddenly made the exhibition room feel familiar.\nWhat was disappointing was that, perhaps because of its popularity, the venue was crowded and I could not take my time looking at the small tools. I would recommend choosing a quiet time, such as a weekday morning. Even people who don't know much about art can enjoy this exhibition with the feeling of peeking into a craft workshop.",
