    "translation": "Many people sigh, \"My forgetfulness is terrible.\" Everyone assumes that the better your memory, the better. But in recent brain science, forgetting is thought of not as a failure of memory but as one of the brain's important functions.\nThe brain receives an enormous amount of information every day. If it stored all of it, retrieving the information you truly need would take too long. In other words, by actively erasing information it does not use, the brain makes important memories easier to find. You could even say that it is because we forget that we are able to think. It may help to picture the mind as a tidy desk on which only the necessary papers are laid out.\nOf course, there are times when forgetting an important appointment causes trouble. But often that is not so much a decline in memory as simply the brain having failed to judge that information as \"important.\" You just need devices that mark it as important for the brain — writing it in a planner, telling someone about it, and so on. Indeed, I have noticed that I almost never forget plans I have written in my planner or promises I have told someone about. Before blaming your \"forgetful self,\" it can't hurt to know what forgetting means.",
