    "translation": "When you hear \"30 percent chance of precipitation,\" do you take an umbrella when you go out? People's judgments differ, but what is interesting is that even with the same content, how it is conveyed changes people's behavior.\nAccording to one survey, more people carried umbrellas when told \"it will rain on 3 out of 10 occasions\" than when told \"there is a 30 percent chance of rain.\" Even though the numbers mean the same thing, an expression that makes it easy to picture a concrete scene is more likely to connect to action. Even when conveying the same fact, a single choice of words can change whether people carry an umbrella — words are fascinating things.\nWeather information tends to be judged only on whether it comes true. But no matter how accurate a forecast is, if the people who hear it do not act, disasters cannot be prevented. Information acquires meaning only once it reaches people and is understood. The fact that in recent years the Meteorological Agency has begun using strong language such as \"heavy rain seen once in decades\" and \"take action to protect your life\" can also be seen as a device to connect forecasts to action. Weather forecasting is a science, and at the same time it is an art of words.",
