    "translation": "Users of audiobooks — books you \"listen to\" — are increasing. Since you can listen while commuting or doing housework, it is a welcome service for people who cannot find time to read. I tried it myself, and the professional narration was more pleasant than I expected; the satisfaction of having \"finished listening to\" a full-length novel was not much different from having finished reading one. My once-boring morning commute had, before I knew it, turned into time when I looked forward to the story's continuation.\nThere is also the opinion that \"just listening with your ears cannot be called reading.\" But if you think about it, stories were originally things to be told aloud. The history of reading by written text is, if anything, the newer one for humankind. As for depth of understanding, there is even research suggesting that, depending on the content, listening sinks in better. There is no reason at all to dismiss reading with the ears as taking the easy way.\nThat said, there are things it suits and things it does not. For practical books full of charts, or difficult books you want to read while flipping back to check earlier pages, paper or a screen is still more convenient. I would like to think of audiobooks not as a substitute for reading, but as a new door that widens the entrance to reading.",
