    "translation": "The cooking show I watch every week, \"Kitchen Time,\" marked ten years on the air this spring. The appeal of this show lies not in the beauty of the finished dishes but in the fact that it does not hide failures. If the instructor burns the eggs, they broadcast it as is, and she continues, \"If it burns, turn off the heat and redo it like this.\" Among the many shows that edit failures out, this is rare. The scene from the very first broadcast ten years ago, when the instructor let a pot boil over, is still fondly retold among viewers.\nIt is said that many viewers are \"watch-only\" — they watch cooking shows but never actually cook. Footage of perfect dishes completed with perfect technique is beautiful, but it somehow feels like someone else's business. This show, by contrast, makes viewers think, \"I could probably manage this.\" In fact, most of the dishes I have learned over the past ten years started with this show. My own cooking, which began with burnt tamagoyaki, has now improved to the point that my family compliments it.\nIf all you want is information and technique, you can learn as much as you like on video sites these days. Even so, I think this show has lasted ten years because it teaches that cooking includes the process of things not going well.",
