    "translation": "More and more art museums are permitting photography of exhibited works. Photos posted to social media serve as publicity, and in many countries overseas, being allowed to photograph is the norm. A photography ban used to be the common sense of Japanese museums, but that common sense is changing. At a special exhibition I visited last month, photography was permitted in certain rooms only, and many people had their cameras out.\nAlongside voices of welcome, there is also criticism: shutter sounds disturb viewing, and people feel satisfied the moment they take a photo and stop really looking at the work. Indeed, the sight of a line forming to photograph a famous painting while no one looks at the painting itself gives one pause. Surely the time spent looking at a work matters more than the record of having seen it.\nStill, I am not opposed to permitting photography itself. One survey found that many people later looked up the works they had photographed. A photo can be not the end of viewing but the beginning of its continuation. A single photo can even become the reason someone visits the next exhibition. It comes down to how it is used. What museums will need from now on are arrangements that respect both those who want to shoot and those who want to look in quiet — for example, separating rooms where photography is allowed from rooms where it is not.",
