    "translation": "Online classes were once thought of as a temporary substitute, but they have now taken root at many universities as a normal option. You can rewatch recordings as many times as you like, no commuting time is needed, and because questions can be sent in writing, students who are uncomfortable speaking up can participate more easily. These advantages cannot be had in a classroom lesson. Indeed, for students whose family or health circumstances make commuting difficult, online classes have become a lifeline for continuing their studies.\nOn the other hand, some things have been lost: the small talk before and after class, the experience of deepening your understanding by happening to overhear the question of the student next to you, the flexibility of an instructor who watches students' faces and changes the explanation. Only by leaving the classroom did we first realize that no small part of learning was actually happening outside the class \"itself.\" Realizing something's value only after losing it applies, it seems, to education as well.\nSo we should stop debating this as a binary choice between in-person and online. Leave the transmission of knowledge to recordings, and use classroom time for discussion and questions. Designing that kind of combination is precisely the task for education from now on.",
